# Frontend Rules

- Use TypeScript strict mode and typed API contracts.
- Treat the backend as the source of truth for identity, permissions, approval state and workflow state.
- Never implement authorization only by hiding or disabling UI elements.
- Keep presentation components separate from data fetching and business rules.
- Provide loading, empty, error, retry and unauthorized states.
- Make approval actions explicit and show the exact action/resource being approved.
- Do not expose secrets or privileged provider credentials in browser code.
- Meet keyboard accessibility, semantic HTML and responsive layout requirements.
- Use the established Luxor9 visual direction: obsidian, mineral ivory and restrained signal cyan; avoid generic chatbot styling and ornamental gradients.
